content:
A \textbf{proposition} (also called a \textit{statement}) is a declarative sentence that
is \textbf{either true or false, but not both}.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item Every proposition has a \textbf{truth value}: either \textbf{True (T)} or \textbf{False (F)}.
    \item Propositions are usually denoted by lowercase letters: $p,\, q,\, r,\, s, \ldots$
    \item Questions, commands, and exclamations are \textbf{NOT} propositions.
    \item A proposition cannot be \textit{sometimes true} — its truth value must be definite.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

Let us form propositions from situations a CS student encounters daily.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
Define the following propositions:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $p$ : ``Today is Friday.'' \hfill \textit{(Truth value depends on the day)}
    \item $q$ : ``The lab is open.'' \hfill \textit{(Truth value depends on schedule)}
    \item $r$ : ``$17$ is a prime number.'' \hfill \textcolor{darkgreen}{True}
    \item $s$ : ``All dogs are cats.'' \hfill \textcolor{red}{False}
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
Each of $p, q, r, s$ is a valid proposition because each is a declarative sentence with a
definite (though sometimes contextual) truth value. We will use $p$ and $q$ throughout
this chapter to build compound statements.

\vspace{0.5cm}

The \textbf{negation} of a proposition $p$, written $\neg p$ (read: ``not $p$''), is the
proposition that is \textbf{true when $p$ is false}, and \textbf{false when $p$ is true}.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textbf{Truth Table for Negation:}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $\neg p$ \\
\hline
T & F \\
\hline
F & T \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $\neg p$ simply \textbf{flips} the truth value of $p$.
    \item Applying negation \textbf{twice} returns the original: $\neg(\neg p) \equiv p$.
    \item In English, negation is expressed by inserting ``not'' or using ``It is not the case that \ldots''
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

The \textbf{conjunction} of propositions $p$ and $q$, written $p \land q$ (read:
``$p$ \textbf{and} $q$''), is true \textbf{only when both $p$ and $q$ are true}.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textbf{Truth Table for Conjunction:}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $q$ & $p \land q$ \\
\hline
T & T & T \\
\hline
T & F & F \\
\hline
F & T & F \\
\hline
F & F & F \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $p \land q$ is true in \textbf{only 1 out of 4} possible cases.
    \item Think of it as a strict ``both must pass'' condition.
    \item In English: ``and'', ``but'', ``however'', ``moreover'' can all represent $\land$.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

The \textbf{disjunction} of propositions $p$ and $q$, written $p \lor q$ (read:
``$p$ \textbf{or} $q$''), is true when \textbf{at least one of $p$ or $q$ is true}.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textbf{Truth Table for Disjunction:}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $q$ & $p \lor q$ \\
\hline
T & T & T \\
\hline
T & F & T \\
\hline
F & T & T \\
\hline
F & F & F \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $p \lor q$ is false in \textbf{only 1 out of 4} possible cases (when both are false).
    \item This is the \textbf{inclusive or} — both can be true simultaneously.
    \item The \textbf{exclusive or} $p \oplus q$ is true only when \textit{exactly one} is true.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

The \textbf{exclusive or} of $p$ and $q$, written $p \oplus q$, is true when
\textbf{exactly one} of $p$ and $q$ is true (but not both).

\vspace{0.4cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $q$ & $p \oplus q$ \\
\hline
T & T & F \\
\hline
T & F & T \\
\hline
F & T & T \\
\hline
F & F & F \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Note:}} In daily English, ``or'' often means exclusive or. For example:
``You can have tea \textbf{or} coffee'' usually means you cannot have both. In mathematics,
however, $\lor$ is always \textit{inclusive or} unless stated otherwise.

\vspace{0.5cm}

The \textbf{conditional statement} ``if $p$ then $q$'', written $p \rightarrow q$, is
false \textbf{only when $p$ is true and $q$ is false}. In all other cases it is true.

\begin{center}
$p$ is called the \textbf{hypothesis} (antecedent) \quad and \quad
$q$ is called the \textbf{conclusion} (consequent).
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Truth Table for Conditional:}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $q$ & $p \rightarrow q$ \\
\hline
T & T & T \\
\hline
T & F & F \\
\hline
F & T & T \\
\hline
F & F & T \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Ways to read $p \rightarrow q$:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item ``If $p$, then $q$''
    \item ``$p$ implies $q$''
    \item ``$p$ only if $q$''
    \item ``$q$ is necessary for $p$''
    \item ``$p$ is sufficient for $q$''
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Important:}} When the hypothesis $p$ is \textbf{false}, the conditional is
\textbf{vacuously true} regardless of $q$. Think: ``If pigs can fly, I will give you \$100.''
Since pigs cannot fly, this promise is never broken — the statement is true.

\vspace{0.5cm}

The \textbf{biconditional} ``$p$ if and only if $q$'', written $p \leftrightarrow q$
(abbreviated \textbf{iff}), is true when $p$ and $q$ have the \textbf{same truth value}.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $q$ & $p \leftrightarrow q$ \\
\hline
T & T & T \\
\hline
T & F & F \\
\hline
F & T & F \\
\hline
F & F & T \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
Note that $p \leftrightarrow q \;\equiv\; (p \rightarrow q) \land (q \rightarrow p)$.
This equivalence appears directly in past paper 2020 Q1(xiv).

\vspace{0.5cm}

Construct the truth table for $\neg p \lor q$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Strategy:} We evaluate column by column — first compute $\neg p$, then
combine with $q$ using $\lor$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $q$ & $\neg p$ & $\neg p \lor q$ \\
\hline
T & T & F & T \\
\hline
T & F & F & F \\
\hline
F & T & T & T \\
\hline
F & F & T & T \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Observation:} Compare this truth table with $p \rightarrow q$:

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $q$ & $\neg p \lor q$ & $p \rightarrow q$ \\
\hline
T & T & T & T \\
\hline
T & F & F & F \\
\hline
F & T & T & T \\
\hline
F & F & T & T \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} The columns for $\neg p \lor q$ and $p \rightarrow q$ are
\textbf{identical}. Therefore:
\[
    \boxed{p \rightarrow q \;\equiv\; \neg p \lor q}
\]
This is one of the most important logical equivalences and will be used in proofs throughout this chapter.

\vspace{0.5cm}

Construct the truth table for $(p \oplus q) \land (p \oplus \neg q)$.\\
\textit{(This exact expression appeared in \textbf{Past Paper 2021 Q3(i)}.)}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 1:} For three variables we would need $2^3 = 8$ rows, but here we have
only $p$ and $q$, so $2^2 = 4$ rows suffice.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 2:} Build columns one operation at a time.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $q$ & $\neg q$ & $p \oplus q$ & $p \oplus \neg q$ & $(p \oplus q)\land(p \oplus \neg q)$ \\
\hline
T & T & F & F & T & F \\
\hline
T & F & T & T & F & F \\
\hline
F & T & F & T & F & F \\
\hline
F & F & T & F & T & F \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} The final column is \textbf{always False}.
Therefore $(p \oplus q) \land (p \oplus \neg q)$ is a \textbf{contradiction}
(a statement that is always false regardless of the truth values of its variables).
\[
    \boxed{(p \oplus q) \land (p \oplus \neg q) \;\equiv\; \mathbf{F}}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Proof:}}

We need to show that $p \rightarrow q$ and $\neg p \lor q$ have identical truth values
for every possible combination of truth values of $p$ and $q$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 1: Enumerate all cases.}

There are $2^2 = 4$ possible truth-value assignments for $(p,\, q)$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 2: Evaluate both sides for each case.}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $q$ & $\neg p$ & $p \rightarrow q$ & $\neg p \lor q$ \\
\hline
T & T & F & T & T \\
\hline
T & F & F & F & F \\
\hline
F & T & T & T & T \\
\hline
F & F & T & T & T \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 3: Compare columns.}

In every row, the column for $p \rightarrow q$ and the column for $\neg p \lor q$
show the \textbf{same truth value}.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} Since $p \rightarrow q$ and $\neg p \lor q$
agree on all possible inputs, by the definition of logical equivalence:
\[
    p \rightarrow q \;\equiv\; \neg p \lor q \qed
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

Given the conditional $p \rightarrow q$, three related propositions are defined:

\vspace{0.4cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Name} & \textbf{Symbolic Form} & \textbf{Read as} \\
\hline
\textbf{Converse} & $q \rightarrow p$ & ``If $q$, then $p$'' \\
\hline
\textbf{Inverse} & $\neg p \rightarrow \neg q$ & ``If not $p$, then not $q$'' \\
\hline
\textbf{Contrapositive} & $\neg q \rightarrow \neg p$ & ``If not $q$, then not $p$'' \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Critical Fact:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item The \textbf{contrapositive} is \textit{always} logically equivalent to the original:
          $\;p \rightarrow q \;\equiv\; \neg q \rightarrow \neg p$
    \item The \textbf{converse} and \textbf{inverse} are \textit{not} equivalent to the original.
    \item The converse and inverse \textit{are} equivalent to each other.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

Let $p$ = ``It is raining'' and $q$ = ``The ground is wet.''
Write the conditional, converse, inverse, and contrapositive.
Then determine which pairs are logically equivalent.

\textit{(Past Paper 2022 Q1(xii) directly tests this concept.)}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{tabular}{|l|p{5.5cm}|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Statement} & \textbf{English} & \textbf{Symbolic} \\
\hline
Conditional & If it is raining, then the ground is wet. & $p \rightarrow q$ \\
\hline
Converse & If the ground is wet, then it is raining. & $q \rightarrow p$ \\
\hline
Inverse & If it is not raining, then the ground is not wet. & $\neg p \rightarrow \neg q$ \\
\hline
Contrapositive & If the ground is not wet, then it is not raining. & $\neg q \rightarrow \neg p$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textbf{Equivalence check:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $p \rightarrow q \;\equiv\; \neg q \rightarrow \neg p$ \hfill \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark\ Equivalent}
    \item $q \rightarrow p \;\equiv\; \neg p \rightarrow \neg q$ \hfill \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark\ Equivalent}
    \item $p \rightarrow q \;\not\equiv\; q \rightarrow p$ \hfill \textcolor{red}{$\times$\ Not Equivalent}
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Intuitive check:} The ground can be wet because a pipe burst — not because of rain.
So the converse being true does not follow from the original conditional being true.

\vspace{0.5cm}





\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=1.5cm,
    box/.style={rectangle, rounded corners, draw=primaryblue, fill=primaryblue!10,
                text width=3.2cm, align=center, minimum height=1cm, font=\small},
    arrow/.style={->, thick, color=accentorange}
]

\node[box, fill=primaryblue!25, text width=4cm, font=\bfseries\small] (center)
    {Propositions\\$p,\; q$};

\node[box, above left=1.2cm and 2.5cm of center]  (neg)
    {Negation\\$\neg p$\\``not $p$''};

\node[box, above right=1.2cm and 2.5cm of center] (conj)
    {Conjunction\\$p \land q$\\``$p$ and $q$''};

\node[box, below left=1.2cm and 2.5cm of center]  (disj)
    {Disjunction\\$p \lor q$\\``$p$ or $q$''};

\node[box, below right=1.2cm and 2.5cm of center] (cond)
    {Conditional\\$p \rightarrow q$\\``if $p$ then $q$''};

\node[box, above=2.4cm of center] (bicond)
    {Biconditional\\$p \leftrightarrow q$\\``$p$ iff $q$''};

\node[box, below=2.4cm of center] (xor)
    {Exclusive Or\\$p \oplus q$\\``exactly one''};

\draw[arrow] (center) -- (neg);
\draw[arrow] (center) -- (conj);
\draw[arrow] (center) -- (disj);
\draw[arrow] (center) -- (cond);
\draw[arrow] (center) -- (bicond);
\draw[arrow] (center) -- (xor);

\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{center}
\textcolor{darkgray}{\small\textit{Figure 1.1: The six fundamental logical connectives built from propositions $p$ and $q$.}}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.5cm}

When parentheses are absent, logical operators are evaluated in the following order
(from highest to lowest precedence):

\vspace{0.4cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Priority} & \textbf{Operator} & \textbf{Symbol} \\
\hline
1 (highest) & Negation & $\neg$ \\
\hline
2 & Conjunction & $\land$ \\
\hline
3 & Disjunction & $\lor$ \\
\hline
4 & Conditional & $\rightarrow$ \\
\hline
5 (lowest) & Biconditional & $\leftrightarrow$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Example:} $\neg p \land q \lor r \rightarrow s$ is parsed as
$\bigl((\neg p) \land q\bigr) \lor r \rightarrow s$,
which equals $\bigl[\bigl((\neg p) \land q\bigr) \lor r\bigr] \rightarrow s$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

Given $p$ = T, $q$ = F, $r$ = T, evaluate $\neg p \lor q \rightarrow p \land r$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 1: Apply precedence to determine grouping.}
\[
    \neg p \lor q \rightarrow p \land r
    \;=\; \bigl[(\neg p) \lor q\bigr] \rightarrow \bigl[p \land r\bigr]
\]

\textbf{Step 2: Substitute truth values.}
\begin{align*}
    \neg p &= \neg\text{T} = \text{F}\\
    \neg p \lor q &= \text{F} \lor \text{F} = \text{F}\\
    p \land r &= \text{T} \land \text{T} = \text{T}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: Evaluate the conditional.}
\[
    \text{F} \rightarrow \text{T} = \textbf{T}
\]

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Final Answer:}} $\boxed{\text{True}}$

\vspace{0.2cm}
(When the hypothesis is False, the conditional is always True — vacuous truth.)

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faLightbulb\ Section 1.1 Summary — Propositions \& Connectives}}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Connective} & \textbf{Symbol} & \textbf{True when\ldots} & \textbf{False when\ldots} \\
\hline
Negation & $\neg p$ & $p$ is F & $p$ is T \\
\hline
Conjunction & $p \land q$ & Both T & At least one F \\
\hline
Disjunction & $p \lor q$ & At least one T & Both F \\
\hline
Exclusive Or & $p \oplus q$ & Exactly one T & Both same \\
\hline
Conditional & $p \rightarrow q$ & $p$ is F, or $q$ is T & $p$ is T and $q$ is F \\
\hline
Biconditional & $p \leftrightarrow q$ & Both same & Different values \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faCheckCircle\ Key Equivalences to Memorise:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $p \rightarrow q \;\equiv\; \neg p \lor q$
    \item $p \rightarrow q \;\equiv\; \neg q \rightarrow \neg p$ \quad (contrapositive)
    \item $p \leftrightarrow q \;\equiv\; (p \rightarrow q) \land (q \rightarrow p)$
    \item $\neg(\neg p) \;\equiv\; p$ \quad (double negation)
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Past Paper Connection:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item \textbf{2020 Q1(v):} Define tautology $\rightarrow$ Section 1.2
    \item \textbf{2021 Q1(ix):} $p \land \neg q$ translation $\rightarrow$ Example 3 above
    \item \textbf{2022 Q1(xii):} Converse \& contrapositive $\rightarrow$ Definition 1.8
    \item \textbf{2024 Q5:} Symbolic representation $\rightarrow$ Example 3 type
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

A \textbf{truth table} is a table that lists all possible truth-value combinations
of the propositional variables in a compound proposition, together with the resulting
truth value of the compound proposition for each combination.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item If a compound proposition contains $n$ distinct variables, its truth table
          has $\mathbf{2^n}$ rows.
    \item Columns are built \textbf{left to right}, one operation at a time, following
          operator precedence.
    \item The \textbf{final column} always corresponds to the entire compound proposition.
    \item Variables are listed with alternating T/F patterns:
          the rightmost variable alternates every row, the next every 2 rows,
          the next every 4 rows, and so on.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Row count rule:}
\[
    \text{Number of rows} = 2^n \quad \text{where } n = \text{number of distinct variables}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

A compound proposition is called a \textbf{tautology} if it is \textbf{true for every
possible truth-value assignment} of its propositional variables.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item A tautology is also called a \textbf{logically valid} statement.
    \item No matter what truth values you assign to its variables, the result is always \textbf{T}.
    \item Tautologies are used as the foundation of valid \textbf{rules of inference}.
    \item Notation: We write $\models \varphi$ to indicate that $\varphi$ is a tautology.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Simple example:} $p \lor \neg p$ is always true (Law of Excluded Middle).

\vspace{0.5cm}

A compound proposition is called a \textbf{contradiction} if it is \textbf{false for every
possible truth-value assignment} of its propositional variables.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item A contradiction is the exact \textbf{opposite} of a tautology.
    \item No matter what truth values you assign, the result is always \textbf{F}.
    \item The negation of a tautology is always a contradiction, and vice versa.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Simple example:} $p \land \neg p$ is always false (Law of Non-Contradiction).

\vspace{0.5cm}

Show that $p \lor \neg p$ is a tautology using a truth table.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
This proposition has $n = 1$ variable, so we need $2^1 = 2$ rows.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $\neg p$ & $p \lor \neg p$ \\
\hline
T & F & T \\
\hline
F & T & T \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} The final column is \textbf{all T}.
Therefore $p \lor \neg p$ is a \textbf{tautology}.
\[
    \boxed{p \lor \neg p \;\equiv\; \mathbf{T}}
\]
This is known as the \textbf{Law of Excluded Middle}: every proposition is either
true or its negation is true --- there is no middle ground.

\vspace{0.5cm}

Show that $p \land \neg p$ is a contradiction using a truth table.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $\neg p$ & $p \land \neg p$ \\
\hline
T & F & F \\
\hline
F & T & F \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} The final column is \textbf{all F}.
Therefore $p \land \neg p$ is a \textbf{contradiction}.
\[
    \boxed{p \land \neg p \;\equiv\; \mathbf{F}}
\]
This is the \textbf{Law of Non-Contradiction}: a proposition and its negation
cannot both be true at the same time.

\vspace{0.5cm}

Using a truth table, show whether the following is a tautology or not:
\[
    (P \leftrightarrow Q) \;\leftrightarrow\; \bigl[(P \rightarrow Q) \land (Q \rightarrow P)\bigr]
\]

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 1: Count rows.} Two variables $P$ and $Q$, so $2^2 = 4$ rows.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 2: Build intermediate columns.}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$P$ & $Q$ & $P \rightarrow Q$ & $Q \rightarrow P$ &
$(P{\rightarrow}Q)\land(Q{\rightarrow}P)$ & $P \leftrightarrow Q$ \\
\hline
T & T & T & T & T & T \\
\hline
T & F & F & T & F & F \\
\hline
F & T & T & F & F & F \\
\hline
F & F & T & T & T & T \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 3: Evaluate the outer biconditional.}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$P$ & $Q$ & $P \leftrightarrow Q$ &
$(P{\leftrightarrow}Q)\leftrightarrow[(P{\rightarrow}Q)\land(Q{\rightarrow}P)]$ \\
\hline
T & T & T & $T \leftrightarrow T = $ \textbf{T} \\
\hline
T & F & F & $F \leftrightarrow F = $ \textbf{T} \\
\hline
F & T & F & $F \leftrightarrow F = $ \textbf{T} \\
\hline
F & F & T & $T \leftrightarrow T = $ \textbf{T} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} All entries in the final column are \textbf{T},
so the statement is a \textbf{tautology}.
\[
    \boxed{(P \leftrightarrow Q) \;\leftrightarrow\; [(P \rightarrow Q) \land (Q \rightarrow P)]
    \;\equiv\; \mathbf{T}}
\]
This confirms the fundamental definition: $p \leftrightarrow q$ means exactly
$(p \rightarrow q) \land (q \rightarrow p)$.

