\vspace{0.5cm}

Show that $(p \land q) \rightarrow p$ is a tautology.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Method 1: Truth Table}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $q$ & $p \land q$ & $(p \land q) \rightarrow p$ \\
\hline
T & T & T & T \\
\hline
T & F & F & T \\
\hline
F & T & F & T \\
\hline
F & F & F & T \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
The final column is \textbf{all T}. \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark\ Tautology confirmed.}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textbf{Method 2: Logical Reasoning (no truth table)}

The conditional $p \land q \rightarrow p$ is false \textit{only} when the hypothesis
$p \land q$ is \textbf{true} and the conclusion $p$ is \textbf{false}.

But if $p \land q$ is true, then by the definition of conjunction, $p$ must be true.
This is a direct contradiction --- the hypothesis being true forces the conclusion to be true.
Therefore the conditional \textbf{can never be false}.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}}
\[
    \boxed{(p \land q) \rightarrow p \;\equiv\; \mathbf{T}}
\]
This is known as the \textbf{Simplification} rule of inference and is used directly
in logical proofs.

