\vspace{0.5cm}

State De Morgan's Laws in set theory and use them to find the negation of:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*), leftmargin=2em]
    \item Carlos will bicycle or run tomorrow.
    \item Ibrahim is smart and hardworking.
\end{enumerate}
\textit{(Part (b) directly from Past Paper 2023 Q1(iv).)}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(a)} Let $p$ = ``Carlos will bicycle'' and $q$ = ``Carlos will run tomorrow.''

Original statement: $p \lor q$

Negation: $\neg(p \lor q) \equiv \neg p \land \neg q$

\textbf{In English:} ``Carlos will not bicycle \textbf{and} will not run tomorrow.''

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} Let $p$ = ``Ibrahim is smart'' and $q$ = ``Ibrahim is hardworking.''

Original statement: $p \land q$

Negation: $\neg(p \land q) \equiv \neg p \lor \neg q$

\textbf{In English:} ``Ibrahim is not smart \textbf{or} is not hardworking.''

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Common Mistake:}} Students often negate
``Ibrahim is smart and hardworking'' as ``Ibrahim is not smart and not hardworking.''
This is \textcolor{red}{wrong} --- the \textbf{and} must change to \textbf{or} when
negating a conjunction (De Morgan's Law).

\vspace{0.5cm}

Simplify $\neg(\neg p \land q) \land (p \lor q)$ using logical laws.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
We apply laws step by step, naming each law used.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{align*}
    &\neg(\neg p \land q) \land (p \lor q) \\
    &\equiv (\neg(\neg p) \lor \neg q) \land (p \lor q)
        &&\text{De Morgan's Law} \\
    &\equiv (p \lor \neg q) \land (p \lor q)
        &&\text{Double Negation Law} \\
    &\equiv p \lor (\neg q \land q)
        &&\text{Distributive Law} \\
    &\equiv p \lor \mathbf{F}
        &&\text{Negation Law: } q \land \neg q \equiv \mathbf{F} \\
    &\equiv p
        &&\text{Identity Law}
\end{align*}

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Result:}}
\[
    \boxed{\neg(\neg p \land q) \land (p \lor q) \;\equiv\; p}
\]

\textit{This is exactly the type of simplification asked in Past Paper 2025 Q1(iv).}

