\vspace{0.5cm}

A \textbf{predicate} is a statement involving one or more variables whose truth value
depends on the value(s) assigned to those variables. A predicate with variable $x$
is written $P(x)$ and is called a \textbf{propositional function}.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $P(x)$ is \textbf{not} a proposition --- it becomes one only when a specific
          value is substituted for $x$.
    \item The set of all allowable values for a variable is called the \textbf{domain}
          (or \textit{universe of discourse}).
    \item Predicates can have more than one variable: $P(x,y)$, $Q(x,y,z)$, etc.
    \item Once all variables are assigned values, $P(x)$ becomes a proposition
          with a definite truth value.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Example:} Let $P(x)$ denote ``$x > 5$.''
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $P(6)$: ``$6 > 5$'' \hfill \textcolor{darkgreen}{True}
    \item $P(3)$: ``$3 > 5$'' \hfill \textcolor{red}{False}
    \item $P(x)$: not yet a proposition (truth value unknown until $x$ is specified)
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

Let $P(x)$ denote the statement ``$x > 5$.''
What are the truth values of $P(5)$ and $P(4)$?

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $P(5)$: Substitute $x = 5$ $\Rightarrow$ ``$5 > 5$'' $\Rightarrow$
          \textcolor{red}{\textbf{False}} \quad (5 is not strictly greater than 5)
    \item $P(4)$: Substitute $x = 4$ $\Rightarrow$ ``$4 > 5$'' $\Rightarrow$
          \textcolor{red}{\textbf{False}} \quad (4 is not greater than 5)
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Answer:}} $P(5) = \mathbf{F}$ and $P(4) = \mathbf{F}$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
For $P(x)$ to be true, we need $x > 5$, i.e., $x \geq 6$ for integer values.
For example, $P(6) = $ True, $P(100) = $ True.

\vspace{0.5cm}

The \textbf{universal quantification} of $P(x)$, written $\forall x\, P(x)$
(read: ``\textbf{for all} $x$, $P(x)$'' or ``\textbf{for every} $x$, $P(x)$''),
is the proposition that claims $P(x)$ is true \textbf{for every element $x$}
in the domain.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Symbol} & $\forall$ (inverted A --- think ``\textbf{A}ll'') \\
\hline
\textbf{Read as} & ``for all'', ``for every'', ``for each'', ``for any'' \\
\hline
\textbf{True when} & $P(x)$ holds for \textbf{every} element in the domain \\
\hline
\textbf{False when} & There exists \textbf{at least one} element where $P(x)$ is false \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item To \textbf{disprove} $\forall x\, P(x)$, it suffices to find \textbf{one}
          element $x_0$ where $P(x_0)$ is false. This element is called a
          \textbf{counterexample}.
    \item $\forall x\, P(x)$ is equivalent to the conjunction of $P(a)$ over
          all elements $a$ in the domain.
    \item The domain must always be specified --- the truth of $\forall x\, P(x)$
          depends heavily on what the domain is.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

The \textbf{existential quantification} of $P(x)$, written $\exists x\, P(x)$
(read: ``\textbf{there exists} an $x$ such that $P(x)$''), is the proposition
that claims $P(x)$ is true for \textbf{at least one element $x$} in the domain.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Symbol} & $\exists$ (reversed E --- think ``\textbf{E}xists'') \\
\hline
\textbf{Read as} & ``there exists'', ``for some'', ``there is at least one'' \\
\hline
\textbf{True when} & $P(x)$ holds for \textbf{at least one} element in the domain \\
\hline
\textbf{False when} & $P(x)$ is false for \textbf{every} element in the domain \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item To \textbf{prove} $\exists x\, P(x)$, it suffices to find \textbf{one}
          specific element $x_0$ where $P(x_0)$ is true. This is called a
          \textbf{witness}.
    \item $\exists x\, P(x)$ is equivalent to the disjunction of $P(a)$ over
          all elements $a$ in the domain.
    \item Past Paper 2021 Q1(i) directly asks: ``Define existential quantification
          with a suitable example.''
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

Define existential quantification with a suitable example.
Also evaluate $\exists x\, P(x)$ where $P(x)$ is ``$x^2 > 10$''
with domain $\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\}$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Definition:} $\exists x\, P(x)$ means ``there exists at least one element
$x$ in the domain for which $P(x)$ is true.''

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Evaluation:} We check $P(x): x^2 > 10$ for each element.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$x$ & $x^2$ & $x^2 > 10$? \\
\hline
1 & 1  & \textcolor{red}{False} \\
\hline
2 & 4  & \textcolor{red}{False} \\
\hline
3 & 9  & \textcolor{red}{False} \\
\hline
4 & 16 & \textcolor{darkgreen}{True} \\
\hline
5 & 25 & \textcolor{darkgreen}{True} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
Since $P(4)$ is true (and $P(5)$ is true), there \textbf{exists} at least one
element making $P(x)$ true. The witness is $x_0 = 4$.

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} $\exists x\, P(x) = \boxed{\mathbf{True}}$

\vspace{0.5cm}

Let $P(x)$ be ``$x^2 \geq x$'' with domain $\mathbb{R}$ (all real numbers).
Determine if $\forall x\, P(x)$ is true or false.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Claim:} $\forall x\, (x^2 \geq x)$ --- ``For every real number $x$,
$x^2 \geq x$.''

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Testing with examples:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $x = 3$: $9 \geq 3$ \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}
    \item $x = 0$: $0 \geq 0$ \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}
    \item $x = -1$: $1 \geq -1$ \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}
    \item $x = 0.5$: $0.25 \geq 0.5$? \textcolor{red}{$\times$ \textbf{False!}}
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Counterexample found:} $x_0 = 0.5$ gives $P(0.5) = $ False.

\textcolor{red}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} $\forall x\, P(x)$ is \textbf{False}.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Note:}} To disprove a universal statement, we only
need \textbf{one} counterexample. We do \textit{not} need to show it fails everywhere.

\vspace{0.5cm}

The \textbf{uniqueness quantifier} $\exists!\, x\, P(x)$ (read: ``there exists a
\textbf{unique} $x$ such that $P(x)$'') asserts that $P(x)$ is true for
\textbf{exactly one} element in the domain.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Example:} $\exists!\, x\, (x^2 = 0)$ over the reals is \textbf{True}
because only $x = 0$ satisfies $x^2 = 0$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

The negation of a quantified statement is found by swapping the quantifier and
negating the predicate:

\vspace{0.4cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Statement} & \textbf{Negation} & \textbf{Read as} \\
\hline
$\forall x\, P(x)$ & $\exists x\, \neg P(x)$ &
``There exists $x$ such that $P(x)$ is false'' \\
\hline
$\exists x\, P(x)$ & $\forall x\, \neg P(x)$ &
``For all $x$, $P(x)$ is false'' \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item These are the \textbf{De Morgan's Laws for quantifiers}.
    \item The negation of ``all students passed'' is ``some student did not pass''
          --- \textbf{not} ``all students did not pass.''
    \item The negation of ``some student failed'' is ``all students passed.''
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Proof of the first law:}}
$\neg\,\forall x\, P(x) \;\equiv\; \exists x\, \neg P(x)$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 1: What does $\neg\,\forall x\, P(x)$ mean?}

$\forall x\, P(x)$ is true \textit{only if} $P(x)$ holds for \textit{every}
element in the domain $D$. Its negation is true when this \textit{fails} ---
i.e., when $P(x)$ is false for \textit{at least one} element.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 2: Express the failure condition.}

$\forall x\, P(x)$ fails $\iff$ there exists some element $x_0 \in D$ with
$P(x_0) = \mathbf{F}$, i.e., $\neg P(x_0) = \mathbf{T}$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 3: Recognise the definition.}

The condition ``there exists $x_0 \in D$ such that $\neg P(x_0)$ is true''
is exactly the definition of $\exists x\, \neg P(x)$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}}
\[
    \neg\,\forall x\, P(x) \;\equiv\; \exists x\, \neg P(x) \qed
\]

\vspace{0.2cm}
The second law $\neg\,\exists x\, P(x) \equiv \forall x\, \neg P(x)$
follows by an identical argument with the roles of $\forall$ and $\exists$
exchanged.

\vspace{0.5cm}

Find the negation of the statement: ``All goats are mammals.''

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 1: Identify the structure.}

Let $G(x)$ = ``$x$ is a goat'' and $M(x)$ = ``$x$ is a mammal.''

The statement is: $\forall x\, (G(x) \rightarrow M(x))$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 2: Apply De Morgan's Law for quantifiers.}
\begin{align*}
    \neg[\forall x\, (G(x) \rightarrow M(x))]
    &\equiv \exists x\, \neg(G(x) \rightarrow M(x)) \\
    &\equiv \exists x\, \neg(\neg G(x) \lor M(x))
        &&[p \rightarrow q \equiv \neg p \lor q] \\
    &\equiv \exists x\, (G(x) \land \neg M(x))
        &&[\text{De Morgan's Law}]
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: Translate back to English.}

$\exists x\, (G(x) \land \neg M(x))$: ``There exists an $x$ that is a goat
\textbf{and} is not a mammal.''

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Answer:}} The negation is:
\[
    \boxed{\text{``There exists a goat that is not a mammal.''}}
\]

\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Common Mistake:}} The negation of ``All goats are mammals''
is \textbf{not} ``All goats are not mammals'' --- that would be a much stronger claim.
The correct negation only requires \textit{one} goat to fail to be a mammal.

\vspace{0.5cm}

When quantifiers appear one after another, they are called \textbf{nested quantifiers}.
The order of different quantifiers \textbf{matters}.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Expression} & \textbf{Meaning} & \textbf{Order matters?} \\
\hline
$\forall x\, \forall y\, P(x,y)$ &
For all $x$ and all $y$, $P(x,y)$ & No (same quantifier) \\
\hline
$\exists x\, \exists y\, P(x,y)$ &
There exist $x$ and $y$ such that $P(x,y)$ & No (same quantifier) \\
\hline
$\forall x\, \exists y\, P(x,y)$ &
For every $x$, there exists a $y$ such that $P(x,y)$ &
\textcolor{red}{Yes} \\
\hline
$\exists x\, \forall y\, P(x,y)$ &
There exists an $x$ such that for all $y$, $P(x,y)$ &
\textcolor{red}{Yes} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Critical Note:}}
$\forall x\, \exists y\, P(x,y)$ and $\exists x\, \forall y\, P(x,y)$
are \textbf{not} equivalent in general.

\textbf{Example with $P(x,y): y > x$ over integers:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $\forall x\, \exists y\, (y > x)$: For every integer, there is a larger one ---
          \textcolor{darkgreen}{True}
    \item $\exists x\, \forall y\, (y > x)$: There is an integer larger than all others ---
          \textcolor{red}{False}
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

Let $S(x)$ = ``$x$ is a student,''
$F(x)$ = ``$x$ is a faculty member,''
$A(x,y)$ = ``$x$ has asked $y$ a question,''
where the domain consists of all people in the school.

Express each statement using quantifiers:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*), leftmargin=2em]
    \item Every student has asked Professor Gross a question.
    \item Some student has not asked any faculty member a question.
    \item There is a faculty member who has asked every other faculty member a question.
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
Let $g$ denote Professor Gross (a constant in the domain).

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(i) Every student has asked Professor Gross a question.}

We need: for all $x$, if $x$ is a student, then $x$ has asked $g$ a question.
\[
    \boxed{\forall x\, \bigl(S(x) \rightarrow A(x,\, g)\bigr)}
\]

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(ii) Some student has not asked any faculty member a question.}

We need: there exists a student $x$ such that for every faculty member $y$,
$x$ has not asked $y$.
\[
    \boxed{\exists x\, \bigl(S(x) \land \forall y\, (F(y) \rightarrow \neg A(x,\, y))\bigr)}
\]

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(iii) There is a faculty member who has asked every other faculty member a question.}

We need: there exists a faculty member $x$ such that for every other faculty member $y$
(i.e., $y \neq x$), $x$ has asked $y$.
\[
    \boxed{\exists x\, \bigl(F(x) \land \forall y\, (F(y) \land y \neq x
    \rightarrow A(x,\, y))\bigr)}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

Translate the statement ``The sum of two positive integers is always positive''
into a logical expression.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 1: Identify variables and predicates.}

Let the domain be all positive integers $\mathbb{Z}^+ = \{1, 2, 3, \ldots\}$.

Let $x$ and $y$ be any two positive integers. The statement says their sum $x + y$
is also a positive integer.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 2: Write the logical expression.}

Since $x, y \in \mathbb{Z}^+$ by assumption of the domain, we need:
\[
    \boxed{\forall x\, \forall y\, \bigl((x > 0) \land (y > 0)
    \rightarrow (x + y > 0)\bigr)}
\]

Or more concisely, if the domain is already restricted to positive integers:
\[
    \forall x\, \forall y\, (x + y > 0)
\]

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Note:}} The domain always matters. Specifying
the domain as $\mathbb{Z}^+$ vs.\ $\mathbb{Z}$ (all integers) changes whether
the statement needs the explicit positivity conditions.

\vspace{0.5cm}

What is the truth value of $\exists x\, \exists y\, P(x,y)$, where $P(x,y)$
means $x \cdot y > 0$ and $x$, $y$ are real numbers?

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
$\exists x\, \exists y\, P(x,y)$ asks: ``Is there at least one pair $(x,y)$ of
real numbers such that $x \cdot y > 0$?''

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Finding a witness:} Take $x = 2$ and $y = 3$.

\[
    x \cdot y = 2 \times 3 = 6 > 0 \quad \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}
\]

Since we found a specific pair $(x,y) = (2,3)$ satisfying $P(x,y)$, the
existential statement is true.

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Answer:}}
\[
    \boxed{\exists x\, \exists y\, P(x,y) = \mathbf{True}}
\]

Identify the free and bound variables in:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*), leftmargin=2em]
    \item $\exists x\, (x + y = 1)$
    \item $\forall x\, \exists y\, (x^2 + y^2 = 1)$
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
A variable is \textbf{bound} if it is governed by a quantifier. Otherwise it is \textbf{free}.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(a)} $\exists x\, (x + y = 1)$:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $x$ is \textbf{bound} by $\exists x$
    \item $y$ is \textbf{free} --- it has no quantifier
\end{itemize}
This is \textbf{not} a proposition (because $y$ is free --- its value is unspecified).

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $\forall x\, \exists y\, (x^2 + y^2 = 1)$:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $x$ is \textbf{bound} by $\forall x$
    \item $y$ is \textbf{bound} by $\exists y$
\end{itemize}
All variables are bound, so this \textbf{is} a proposition. Over the reals, it is
\textcolor{darkgreen}{True} (for any $x$ with $|x| \leq 1$, we can find $y = \sqrt{1-x^2}$).

\vspace{0.5cm}




