\vspace{0.5cm}

An \textbf{argument} in propositional logic is a sequence of propositions.
All propositions except the last are called \textbf{premises} (or hypotheses),
and the last proposition is the \textbf{conclusion}.

\vspace{0.4cm}
An argument is \textbf{valid} if whenever all its premises are true, the conclusion
is also true. Formally, the argument with premises $p_1, p_2, \ldots, p_n$
and conclusion $q$ is valid if and only if:
\[
    (p_1 \land p_2 \land \cdots \land p_n) \rightarrow q
    \quad \text{is a \textbf{tautology}.}
\]

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item A valid argument does \textbf{not} guarantee that the conclusion is true ---
          only that \textit{if the premises are true, the conclusion must be true}.
    \item An argument can be valid even if some premises are false.
    \item The standard notation for an argument is:
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cl}
& $p_1$ \\
& $p_2$ \\
& $\vdots$ \\
& $p_n$ \\
\hline
$\therefore$ & $q$
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.2cm}
The symbol $\therefore$ means ``therefore.''

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Proof (by Truth Table):}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
We verify that the formula is a tautology.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $q$ & $p \rightarrow q$ & $p \land (p \rightarrow q)$ &
$[p \land (p \rightarrow q)] \rightarrow q$ \\
\hline
T & T & T & T & \textbf{T} \\
\hline
T & F & F & F & \textbf{T} \\
\hline
F & T & T & F & \textbf{T} \\
\hline
F & F & T & F & \textbf{T} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
All entries in the final column are \textbf{T}.

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} Modus Ponens is a tautology, confirming
it is a valid rule of inference. \qed

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{Intuition:} If we know ``$p$'' is true and we know ``if $p$ then $q$,''
then $q$ \textit{must} be true. This is the most natural step in any logical argument.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Proof (using logical equivalences):}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 1:} Recall that $p \rightarrow q \equiv \neg q \rightarrow \neg p$
(contrapositive law).

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 2:} Modus Tollens has the form:
\begin{center}
$\neg q \quad$ and $\quad p \rightarrow q \quad \therefore \quad \neg p$
\end{center}

\textbf{Step 3:} Replace $p \rightarrow q$ with its contrapositive $\neg q \rightarrow \neg p$:
\begin{center}
$\neg q \quad$ and $\quad \neg q \rightarrow \neg p \quad \therefore \quad \neg p$
\end{center}

\textbf{Step 4:} This is exactly \textbf{Modus Ponens} applied to $\neg q$ and
$\neg q \rightarrow \neg p$, which we already proved is valid.

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} Modus Tollens is valid. \qed

\vspace{0.5cm}

State the Modus Ponens rule of inference and apply it to the following:

Premise 1: ``If it is raining, then I will carry an umbrella.''\\
Premise 2: ``It is raining.''\\
Conclusion: ?

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 1: Identify propositions.}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item Let $p$ = ``It is raining''
    \item Let $q$ = ``I will carry an umbrella''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 2: Map to Modus Ponens form.}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cl}
Premise 1: & $p \rightarrow q$ \\
Premise 2: & $p$ \\
\hline
$\therefore$ & $q$
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Step 3: Apply the rule.}

Since we have $p$ (it is raining) and $p \rightarrow q$ (if raining then umbrella),
by Modus Ponens we conclude $q$.

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} ``I will carry an umbrella.''
\[
    \boxed{q : \text{``I will carry an umbrella.''}}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

Define the Modus Tollens rule of inference and apply it:

Premise 1: ``If Aslam studies hard, then he will pass.''\\
Premise 2: ``Aslam did not pass.''\\
Conclusion: ?

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Modus Tollens:} From $p \rightarrow q$ and $\neg q$, conclude $\neg p$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 1: Assign propositions.}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $p$ = ``Aslam studies hard''
    \item $q$ = ``Aslam will pass''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 2: Map to rule form.}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cl}
Premise 1: & $p \rightarrow q$ \\
Premise 2: & $\neg q$ \\
\hline
$\therefore$ & $\neg p$
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} ``Aslam did not study hard.''
\[
    \boxed{\neg p : \text{``Aslam did not study hard.''}}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

Identify the rule used and verify it:

\[
    p \rightarrow q, \quad q \rightarrow r \quad \therefore \quad p \rightarrow r
\]

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Rule identified:} \textbf{Hypothetical Syllogism} --- if $p$ implies $q$
and $q$ implies $r$, then $p$ implies $r$ (transitivity of implication).

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{English example:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $p \rightarrow q$: ``If I study, then I understand the material.''
    \item $q \rightarrow r$: ``If I understand the material, then I pass the exam.''
    \item $\therefore\; p \rightarrow r$: ``If I study, then I pass the exam.''
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Verification by truth table} ($2^3 = 8$ rows):

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $q$ & $r$ & $p{\rightarrow}q$ & $q{\rightarrow}r$ &
$(p{\rightarrow}q)\land(q{\rightarrow}r)\rightarrow(p{\rightarrow}r)$ \\
\hline
T & T & T & T & T & T \\
\hline
T & T & F & T & F & T \\
\hline
T & F & T & F & T & T \\
\hline
T & F & F & F & T & T \\
\hline
F & T & T & T & T & T \\
\hline
F & T & F & T & F & T \\
\hline
F & F & T & T & T & T \\
\hline
F & F & F & T & T & T \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} All T --- Hypothetical Syllogism is a tautology.
\[
    \boxed{(p \rightarrow q) \land (q \rightarrow r) \rightarrow (p \rightarrow r)
    \;\equiv\; \mathbf{T}}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

Give examples of the Addition rule and the Simplification rule of inference.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Addition Rule:} From $p$, conclude $p \lor q$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Form:}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cl}
Premise: & $p$ \\
\hline
$\therefore$ & $p \lor q$
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Example:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item Premise: ``It is raining.''
    \item Conclusion: ``It is raining \textbf{or} the ground is dry.''
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Note:}} The added proposition $q$ can be \textit{anything}
--- even something false or unrelated. The conclusion is still valid because
if $p$ is true, then $p \lor q$ is certainly true.

\vspace{0.5cm}
\textbf{Simplification Rule:} From $p \land q$, conclude $p$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Form:}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cl}
Premise: & $p \land q$ \\
\hline
$\therefore$ & $p$
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Example:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item Premise: ``It is raining \textbf{and} it is cold.''
    \item Conclusion: ``It is raining.''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Verification (Simplification):}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$p$ & $q$ & $p \land q$ & $(p \land q) \rightarrow p$ \\
\hline
T & T & T & T \\
\hline
T & F & F & T \\
\hline
F & T & F & T \\
\hline
F & F & F & T \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
All T --- \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} Simplification is valid.

\vspace{0.5cm}

Show that the following argument is valid and find the conclusion:

\vspace{0.3cm}
Premises:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*), leftmargin=2em]
    \item It is not sunny this afternoon and it is colder than yesterday.
    \item We will go swimming only if it is sunny.
    \item If we do not go swimming, then we will take a canoe trip.
    \item If we take a canoe trip, then we will be home by sunset.
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Step 1: Assign propositions.}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $p$ = ``It is sunny this afternoon''
    \item $q$ = ``It is colder than yesterday''
    \item $r$ = ``We will go swimming''
    \item $s$ = ``We will take a canoe trip''
    \item $t$ = ``We will be home by sunset''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 2: Translate premises.}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*), leftmargin=2em]
    \item $\neg p \land q$
    \item $r \rightarrow p$
    \item $\neg r \rightarrow s$
    \item $s \rightarrow t$
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Step 3: Apply rules of inference step by step.}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Step} & \textbf{Statement} & \textbf{Justification} \\
\hline
5 & $\neg p$ & Simplification from (1): $\neg p \land q \Rightarrow \neg p$ \\
\hline
6 & $\neg r$ & Modus Tollens from (2) and (5): $r\rightarrow p$, $\neg p \Rightarrow \neg r$ \\
\hline
7 & $s$ & Modus Ponens from (3) and (6): $\neg r \rightarrow s$, $\neg r \Rightarrow s$ \\
\hline
8 & $t$ & Modus Ponens from (4) and (7): $s \rightarrow t$, $s \Rightarrow t$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} $\boxed{t}$ --- ``We will be home by sunset.''

\vspace{0.5cm}

In a Wumpus world, we know the following facts:

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*), leftmargin=2em]
    \item There is no Wumpus in room $(1,1)$.
    \item There is no pit adjacent to room $(1,1)$ (no Breeze in $(1,1)$).
    \item If there is a Wumpus in a room, then adjacent rooms have Stench.
    \item If there is a pit in a room, then adjacent rooms have Breeze.
    \item Room $(1,2)$ is adjacent to $(1,1)$.
\end{enumerate}

Use rules of inference to show that room $(1,2)$ is \textbf{safe}
(no Wumpus, no Pit).

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Assign propositions:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $W_{ij}$ = ``There is a Wumpus in room $(i,j)$''
    \item $P_{ij}$ = ``There is a Pit in room $(i,j)$''
    \item $S_{ij}$ = ``There is Stench in room $(i,j)$''
    \item $B_{ij}$ = ``There is Breeze in room $(i,j)$''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Known facts:}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*), leftmargin=2em]
    \item $\neg W_{11}$ (no Wumpus in (1,1))
    \item $\neg B_{11}$ (no Breeze in (1,1))
    \item $W_{12} \rightarrow S_{11}$ (Wumpus in (1,2) $\Rightarrow$ Stench in (1,1))
    \item $P_{12} \rightarrow B_{11}$ (Pit in (1,2) $\Rightarrow$ Breeze in (1,1))
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Step} & \textbf{Statement} & \textbf{Justification} \\
\hline
5 & $\neg S_{11}$ & From (1): no Wumpus anywhere near (1,1) \\
\hline
6 & $\neg W_{12}$ & Modus Tollens: (3) $W_{12}{\rightarrow}S_{11}$, $\neg S_{11}$ \\
\hline
7 & $\neg P_{12}$ & Modus Tollens: (4) $P_{12}{\rightarrow}B_{11}$, $\neg B_{11}$ \\
\hline
8 & $\neg W_{12} \land \neg P_{12}$ & Conjunction of (6) and (7) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} Room $(1,2)$ has neither a Wumpus nor a Pit.
\[
    \boxed{\neg W_{12} \land \neg P_{12} \quad \Rightarrow \quad \text{Room }(1,2)
    \text{ is \textbf{safe}.}}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

Define the conjunction rule of inference. Apply it to the following:

Premise 1: ``Aslam is good in Maths.''\\
Premise 2: ``Aslam is a CS student.''\\
Conclusion: ?

Let $m$ = ``Aslam is good in Maths'' and $c$ = ``Aslam is a CS student.''

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Conjunction Rule:} If $p$ is true and $q$ is true, we may conclude $p \land q$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cl}
Premise 1: & $m$ \\
Premise 2: & $c$ \\
\hline
$\therefore$ & $m \land c$
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Conclusion:}}
\[
    \boxed{m \land c : \text{``Aslam is good in Maths \textbf{and} is a CS student.''}}
\]

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{Verification:}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$m$ & $c$ & $(m \land c)$ (from $m$ and $c$) \\
\hline
T & T & T \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
When both premises are true, the conjunction is true. \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

